WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.140
A Bash script should invoke SSH in a way that is clear to review.

2
00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:14.760
The executable, stable options, destination values, identity file choices, and remote operands should be visible as separate parts of the invocation.

3
00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:19.820
That is much easier to inspect than one large command string assembled from variables.

4
00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:25.380
Arrays are useful for SSH options because they preserve separate argument values.

5
00:00:25.480 --> 00:00:36.600
A script can define fixed options, add validated optional values, and pass the array to SSH without asking Bash to reinterpret a generated command line.

6
00:00:36.700 --> 00:00:41.180
This keeps option boundaries visible and reduces accidental shell interpretation.

7
00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:49.700
Values such as host names, user names, ports, identity file paths, and remote paths should be validated before invocation.

8
00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:53.000
Validation defines what the workflow supports.

9
00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:58.220
Quoting and arrays preserve accepted values as the script passes them to SSH.

10
00:00:58.320 --> 00:00:59.760
Both layers matter.

11
00:01:00.040 --> 00:01:02.680
Avoid unnecessary re-parsing.

12
00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:10.780
If a script stores a full SSH command in a string and later evaluates it, reviewers must reason about another layer of shell parsing.

13
00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:15.600
That increases fragility and makes it easier for data to become syntax.

14
00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:19.940
Known command structure with separate arguments is usually the safer design.

15
00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:23.260
Destination control is especially important.

16
00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:32.560
An input value should not freely decide arbitrary user, host, port, and option behavior unless that is the explicit and reviewed purpose of the tool.

17
00:01:32.660 --> 00:01:38.980
In most automation, a validated environment or target label should map to known destinations and options.

18
00:01:39.260 --> 00:01:44.540
The result is SSH invocation that is testable and maintainable.

19
00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:53.520
Future reviewers can see what can vary, what is fixed, what is validated, and which assumptions belong to the local Bash script before any remote action begins.
